{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey security deposit transfer, interest and penalty rules","requirement":"When rental property is sold, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and remains responsible for investing and returning the deposit; willfully withholding the deposit of an assisted tenant carries a civil penalty.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; the penalty rule applies to tenants who received State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"The civil penalty applies where the tenant received State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense for willfully withholding an assisted tenant's deposit; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense punishable by a $200 fine or 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent-control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after completion of construction, and rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"built_within_years","role":"exempt","years":30,"basis":"construction"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building"]},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years after construction; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent-increase notice and unconscionability standard","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; to increase rent the landlord must terminate the old lease and offer a new lease with proper written notice, and the increase must not be unconscionable or violate any municipal rent control ordinance.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey; increases must comply with municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Condominium and cooperative conversion rent-increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion; increases for new services or amenities are still allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in the converted building, regardless of eligibility for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good-cause eviction requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, written or oral, only for good cause proven in court; the landlord must first serve a Notice to Quit and, where required, a Notice to Cease.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; a landlord may recover possession only by tenant consent or through the legal process of eviction.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"A Notice to Quit is required for every good-cause ground except nonpayment of rent, and each ground except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in the written notice.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","n_rules":5,"note":null}

